# POSIX programs from zosh (TASK-18 step 10, ADR-0153 rule 10): the POSIX
# image without its session file, so init starts zosh with the broker
# beside it. A program of /bin without the native mark runs through the
# broker with the toolchain set, a /tmp of its line's own and the line's
# streams as fds 0, 1 and 2 (ADR-0152 rule 7).
! panicked
! ZORAYA-FAULT
! -FAIL
< Type help to begin
< \r\n~>
# A POSIX program by shell.path, and one in a pipeline after a builtin.
> uname\r
< \r\nZoraya
< \r\n~>
> echo three | wc -c\r
< \r\n6
< \r\n~>
# tcc's description is the system image's (INV-0153-6): -o designates the
# name to create, and grants shows the toolchain set.
> echo 'int main(void) { return 7; }' > seven.c\r
< \r\n~>
> grants tcc seven.c -o seven\r
< tcc gets (a POSIX program):
< ~/seven.c
< /bin /usr /etc       read (system)
< change anything in (this line's own)
< \r\n~>
> tcc seven.c -o seven\r
< \r\n~>
> ./seven; echo after\r
< (the rest of the line was not run)
x \nafter\r
< \r\n~>
> status\r
< exit status 7
< \r\n~>
# INV-0153-7, libposix's half from zosh: a path outside the table twice
# and a missing one inside; one report, and none for the missing one.
> toybox cat ../hello.txt ../hello.txt missing.txt\r
< cat: missing.txt: No such file or directory
<   hint: toybox tried to read /hello.txt, which it was not given\r\n  to allow it: +r / toybox cat
x tried to read ~/missing.txt
< \r\n~>
# The processes of one line share its /tmp; the next line's is new.
> echo 'echo x > /tmp/f; toybox ls /tmp; echo dash says $1' > t.sh\r
< \r\n~>
> dash t.sh hi\r
< \r\nf\r\ndash says hi
x hint
< \r\n~>
> toybox ls /tmp; echo listed\r
< \r\nlisted
x \nf\r
< \r\n~>
# Ctrl-Z of a POSIX job goes through the broker (ADR-0158 rule 4); jobs
# reads the stop from its process job; fg continues it and Ctrl-C ends it.
> toybox sleep 30\r
~ 1500 zzz
> \x1a
< ^Z
< [1] stopped: toybox sleep 30
< \r\n~>
> jobs\r
< [1]  stopped   toybox sleep 30
< \r\n~>
> fg\r
< toybox sleep 30
~ 500 zzz
> \x03
< toybox sleep 30: interrupted (Ctrl-C)
< \r\n~>
# INV-0200-2 (ADR-0200 item 2): a line mixing a native program and a POSIX
# program that catches SIGTSTP. Ctrl-Z suspends the native element's own
# job and sends SIGTSTP through the broker, whose handler runs: it says it
# restores the terminal, then stops. It cannot print once stopped, so its
# line comes before it is, and before the shell says the line stopped.
! tstp: continued\r\ntstp: continued
> requests a | t_posix tstp\r
w a: ready
w tstp: ready
o a: ready
o tstp: ready
> \x1a
< ^Z
< tstp: restoring the terminal
< [1] stopped: requests a | t_posix tstp
< \r\n~> 
o tstp: restoring the terminal
x tstp: continued
> jobs\r
< [1]  stopped   requests a | t_posix tstp
< \r\n~> 
# fg continues both; the POSIX program's handler takes the signal again.
> fg\r
< requests a | t_posix tstp
< tstp: continued
> \x03
< ^C
< (Ctrl-C again to kill it)
> \x03
< ^C
< requests a | t_posix tstp: interrupted (Ctrl-C)
< \r\n~> 
# INV-0200-2's other half: an element that ignores SIGTSTP is not stopped
# within half a second; the shell says so and the line stays in front.
> t_posix notstp\r
w notstp: ready
> \x1a
< ^Z
< did not stop: t_posix notstp
> \x03
< t_posix: interrupted (Ctrl-C)
< \r\n~> 
> poweroff\r
